WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.743
Mission intake captures the request before resources are committed.

00:00:03.743 --> 00:00:08.601
It should identify the requester, purpose, decision need, location, timeframe, required data,

00:00:08.601 --> 00:00:12.648
urgency, privacy impact, deliverable, funding or authority, and known constraints.

00:00:12.648 --> 00:00:17.506
An approval workflow tests whether the mission is lawful, necessary, feasible, proportionate,

00:00:17.506 --> 00:00:19.125
and within organizational capability.

00:00:19.125 --> 00:00:21.554
Safety, aviation, site, privacy, legal, security,

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:25.602
and data owners may review different parts according to risk.

00:00:25.602 --> 00:00:31.269
Scope defines what the crew will fly, observe, and deliver, along with explicit exclusions.

00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:34.912
A bounded scope prevents mission creep and unnecessary collection.

00:00:34.912 --> 00:00:40.579
Changes should return through a documented approval path rather than being improvised during flight.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:42.198
Roles must be clear.

00:00:42.198 --> 00:00:45.031
The pilot in command holds flight-safety authority.

00:00:45.031 --> 00:00:47.865
Visual observers support airspace and hazard awareness.

00:00:47.865 --> 00:00:49.484
Payload operators manage collection.

00:00:49.484 --> 00:00:51.103
Mission leads coordinate objectives.

00:00:51.103 --> 00:00:52.722
Data reviewers validate products.

00:00:52.722 --> 00:00:54.341
Maintainers control airworthiness records.

00:00:54.341 --> 00:00:58.389
Escalation paths cover rule ambiguity, weather, equipment defects, privacy concerns,

00:00:58.389 --> 00:01:01.628
emergency events, data exposure, and pressure to continue.

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:07.699
Staff need permission to pause or reject a mission without being overruled by scheduling pressure.

00:01:07.699 --> 00:01:12.557
The operating model should define handoffs and records from intake through closure.

00:01:12.557 --> 00:01:17.819
Governance succeeds when every important decision has an owner, evidence, and review path,

00:01:17.819 --> 00:01:21.867
while the pilot retains the authority to stop unsafe flight.

00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:26.319
Set realistic service levels for intake, ordinary review, urgent escalation, scheduling,

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:27.939
data delivery, and correction.

00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:32.796
Clear expectations prevent requesters from bypassing governance because they assume the approved

00:01:32.796 --> 00:01:34.820
process will be too slow.
